.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct \- type-inferred hash map construction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct(hm_name, key_type, val_type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred macro that creates a hash map with the default settings.
It works out the key and value types and also creates type
marker variables, which is how the other type-dispatching macros know
the declared key and value types at each call site.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name   Name of the hash map variable to create
key_type  Type of keys
val_type  Type of values
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program if the allocation fails
Creates hidden type marker variables that the other type-dispatching
macros read, and chmap_redeclare(3) creates them again in another scope
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
Uses the default memory management and the default hashing
An array key or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
a key or a value by assignment and C cannot assign an array, so the map
would store the address of the caller's array instead of its elements. A
struct that holds the array works
.fi
.SH "FLOAT AND DOUBLE KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a float or double key_type, two keys are equal when their bits are
equal, and IEEE 754 == plays no part, with one exception: -0.0 and 0.0
are made into the same key, which matches -0.0 == 0.0 in C.
Every other bit pattern, NaN included, is compared bit by bit, so a NaN
key is always found again by the exact bit pattern it was inserted with
(in C, NaN == NaN is always false). Two different NaN payloads are two
different keys.
.fi
.SH "LONG DOUBLE KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a long double key_type, key equality compares the numeric value
(native ==) instead of the bits. -0.0L and 0.0L are the same key, and
two keys with the same value stay one key even when their
platform-defined padding bits differ. Every NaN long double is the same
key, unlike float and double, where each distinct NaN payload is a
different key.
None of this value-based handling applies when a custom hashing
function is given at creation time; see chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH "STRUCT KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a key type the map doesn't know, such as a struct, key equality
compares every byte of the key, padding included, and the default hash
reads every byte too. With GCC 11 or later the macros zero the padding
of their own copy of a key. With any other compiler that padding is
unspecified, so a padded struct key needs a custom hashing function and
a custom key equality function, which you give through
chmap_construct_full(3). See chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, char*);
int key = 42;
chmap_insert(map, key, "hello");
chmap_destroy(map);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct_full (3),
.BR chmap_create (3)
